A||||6||Coop88||High-Beam Indicator||||5||
Z||000000||Coop88||05-04-2001||01:21 AM||||When my headlights are on, my high beam indicator glows. If I turn the high beams on, it lights up to full brilliance. It only does this when the headlights are on, not when I just turn on the running lights. It makes no difference if the ignition is on or off. Could this be just a switch problem? High beam switch or headlight switch? Something else? Car is a formula.<P>Thanks,<BR>Coop||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||05-04-2001||06:43 AM||||Fieros have lots of wierd elecrical gremlins. Check to make sure your headlite/tailite circuit has good grounds all around. Look for screws in those areas with loop connectors screwed into the metal subframe all around. loosen them, move em around a little and retighten. If there all good, might possibly be a switch internal problem, or worse, problem like a short in steering column wiring/switch.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||05-04-2001||06:45 AM||||First thought would be light bleed in the cluster ,but if it doesn't do this with the parking lights it must be the switch. Check the connector and wiring to the switch.<P>Hummm, Rogers post wasn't here when I posted. It makes more sense that it,s in the column. <p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 05-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||05-04-2001||07:42 AM||||This happens a lot to classic cars. I have personally fix the problem in at least four cars: 2 - 69 Firebirds, 1 - 68 Camaro, and 1 - 70 Monte Carlo. In my experience, it's always a bad dash ground. <P>In the case of the 70 Monte Carlo, the grounds were through the mylar circuit board on the back of the speedo. The corrosion in the dash lights and contacts were causing all kinds of strange things to happen. After I pull all the dash lights out, I removed all the lamps, cleaned the sockets, cleaned the contacts, and cleaned the circuit board, loosened and re-tightened all the gauge wiring, and reassembled the dash. After that, it worked perfectly. <P>Good Luck, <BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000004||Coop88||05-05-2001||05:08 AM||||I'm pretty sure I found it. I noticed that my driver's headlight was a dull yellow, instead of the bright white. If I hold the wire that goes to it a certain way, it lights up completely. Anyway, when the light is dull yellow, my high beam indicator glows. When it is at full brilliance, the high beam indicator does not glow. Looks like just a short in the connection. By the way, are the sockets for the headlights supposed to look melted? Is that some kind of grease I see down in the sockets, or plastic?||||reg||3
Z||000005||rogergarrison||05-05-2001||05:53 PM||||yes they put dialelectric grease in the sockets to keep moisture out of them. Plastic part should not be deformed, if it is replace it and put a dab of the grease in each of the three contacts.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-06-2001||01:40 PM||||The grease belongs there. If you need to clean it out then it must be replaced or the connector can corrode quickly.<P>Take the connector off the lamp and inspect the 3 terminals on the lamp. Usually when I find what appears to be a loose socket, one or more of the joints have cracked where the flat lug is soldered to the round pin on the back of the lamp. The connectors are actually pretty solid.<P>If one of the flat terminals is loose, just replace the lamp. You might be able to resolder it, but most times it won't hold very long. Forget soldering if it's one of the halogen lamps with a plastic shell.<P>In your case I'd say the common (ground) terminal cracked. Usually if one breaks the others are likely to do so in future.<P>There are tips to make replacing the lamp easier in my cave.||||reg||1
